  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nOverall weather pattern at the moment depicts a stagnant, orphaned \nupper low over Southern California, and ridging heights aloft\nacross much of the southern Rockies and Texas. At the surface,\nhigh pressure remains fixed over much of the southern Plains,\nextending west into Southern New Mexico and Far West Texas. \n\nEast flow and cool October temperatures will continue into \nSaturday behind last Thursday night's strong cold front. We may be\nin for the coolest night of the season so far tonight, dipping to\nthe upper 30s/lower 40s across much of the forecast area Saturday\nmorning. Temperatures will remain quite cool Saturday afternoon, \nwith lowland highs only reaching the lower 60s. This is 10-15 \ndegrees below normal for mid-October. Winds will be lighter as the\nsurface high drifts east away from our area, generally 10 to 15\nmph with some lingering gusty conditions along the AZ/NM state\nline.\n\nThe temperature rebound begins on Sunday as cold air advection \nfrom the east lessens. Lowland highs should reach the upper\n60s/lower 70s, an 8 to 10-degree warmup from Saturday. Moisture\nincreases out of the south may lead to more cloud coverage Sunday\nafternoon, and maybe even some light rain showers across Southwest\nNew Mexico in the evening and overnight hours. No significant\nrains are expected here.\n\nOrphaned low finally opens up into a wave on Monday, progressing\neast across the southern Rockies. Precipitable water 0.8-1.0\" and\na slightly stronger 50-knot jet aloft should lead to scattered\nrain showers and a few weak thunderstorms. Rain chances seem quite\nspotty with this event, and any rain that does fall looks to be in\nthe T-0.10\" range. No flood risk here.\n\nLate week weather pattern favors a strong warmup in temperatures,\nreturning the seasonal averages (maybe even a bit warmer than \nnormal) Tuesday/Wednesday. Models seem confidence in strong, \nmeridional jet flow diving down the Pacific seaboard midweek, \nsuggesting the development of a deep Pacific low and thus a \nfavorable pattern for rain chances. Best chances looks to play \nout around the Wednesday-Saturday timeframe, but it honestly looks\nlike we're facing multiple rounds of rain chances with a train of\nPacific low pressure systems affecting much of the Southwestern \nUnited States. While QPF in late October will be generally lower \nthan September due to the end of monsoonal flow, less convective \npotential, and speed of these systems, there is still a risk of \nlocalized flash flooding. Any heavy rains that fall will occur \nover saturated soils from the past few weeks.",
